+  # Error raised when the filesystem refuses the path an entry extracts to.
+  # Usually the entry's own name is what makes the path unusable - it is too
+  # long for this filesystem, it carries a byte the filesystem cannot hold, or
+  # a directory it needs is already there as a file.
+  #
+  # The boundary is the destination rather than the cause, though: a refusal
+  # becomes this error whenever `destination_directory` is a directory when it
+  # happens, which includes a directory this process may not write into or may
+  # not traverse. A destination that is missing, or under a path that cannot be
+  # traversed, reports with the operating system's own error instead, because
+  # then it is the caller's argument that is wrong rather than the archive; and
+  # a destination that exists but is not a directory raises nothing at all,
+  # being refused by the containment check before any write is attempted.
+  class ExtractionError < Error
+    # The name of the entry that could not be extracted.
+    attr_reader :entry_name
+
+    # The path that the filesystem refused.
+    attr_reader :path
+
+    # What the filesystem said about it.
+    attr_reader :reason
+
+    # Create a new ExtractionError for the entry named +entry_name+, the
+    # +path+ the filesystem refused, and the +reason+ it gave.
+    def initialize(entry_name, path, reason)
+      super()
+      @entry_name = entry_name
+      @path = path
+      @reason = reason
+    end
+
